# MicroJS8 — QDX USB serial port stable name + autostart trigger
# =====================================================================
#
# The QDX exposes TWO CDC-ACM virtual serial ports:
#   Interface 0 (if00)  CAT control     ← what we want
#   Interface 1 (if01)  debug / terminal
#
# We match interface 0 only and create /dev/qdx pointing at it. The
# kernel also creates /dev/serial/by-id/usb-QRP_Labs_QDX_Transceiver-if00
# automatically for any USB serial device — that's the canonical
# stable name and what rigctld.service actually opens. /dev/qdx is a
# convenience for shell users.
#
# Lifecycle integration with rigctld
# ----------------------------------
# Two complementary actions:
#
#   ADD (QDX appears):
#     TAG+="systemd" + ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="rigctld.service" tells
#     systemd to start (or restart) rigctld whenever the QDX is
#     plugged in or comes back from a USB glitch.
#
#   REMOVE (QDX disappears):
#     RUN+="systemctl stop rigctld" tells systemd to terminate rigctld
#     immediately. Without this, rigctld stays running with a dead
#     serial port: hamlib's design holds the file descriptor and
#     answers some commands from cache, leaving systemd reporting
#     "active" while CAT is actually broken. The next ADD then bounces
#     us through a clean start cycle.
#
# This was discovered the hard way — power-cycling the QDX while the
# Pi stayed up left rigctld in a zombie state where systemctl status
# said "active (running)" but rigctl commands either hung or returned
# stale cached data. Even a libhamlib `\chk_vfo` keepalive succeeded
# from the cache. The combination of (a) explicit stop-on-remove here
# and (b) using a real CAT command (`f`) for the keepalive in
# rigctl_client.py gives us reliable detection + recovery.
#
# USB IDs verified on hardware:
#   idVendor   = 0483  (STMicroelectronics)
#   idProduct  = a34c  (QRP Labs QDX Transceiver — ALL revisions)
#
# (The earlier 0483:5740 — STM32 generic Virtual COM — was wrong; QDX
# firmware uses its own assigned PID.)
#
# Group "dialout" gives the microjs8 user read/write without 0666.

# ADD: create the symlink, fix permissions, and ask systemd to start
# rigctld. Matches on the tty SUBSYSTEM with USB attributes; this rule
# fires when the kernel's CDC-ACM driver creates the ttyACMx device
# node for QDX interface 0.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ACTION=="add", \
    ATTRS{idVendor}=="0483", ATTRS{idProduct}=="a34c", \
    ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="00", \
    SYMLINK+="qdx", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", \
    TAG+="systemd", ENV{SYSTEMD_WANTS}+="rigctld.service"

# REMOVE: tell systemd to stop rigctld. We use --no-block so udev
# isn't held up waiting for systemd; rigctld will be cleanly stopped
# on its own schedule. The full path to systemctl is required because
# udev's RUN environment is minimal.
#
# We can't match on ATTRS here — those come from sysfs which is gone
# by the time the remove event fires. ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID} and
# ENV{ID_MODEL_ID} are populated from earlier add-time properties
# captured by udev's database (cached for the lifetime of the device
# node), so they're available on remove.
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ACTION=="remove", \
    ENV{ID_VENDOR_ID}=="0483", ENV{ID_MODEL_ID}=="a34c", \
    ENV{ID_USB_INTERFACE_NUM}=="00", \
    RUN+="/bin/systemctl --no-block stop rigctld.service"
